---
// Operator disclosure (#316). SynthBench is run by the company that also
// ships Althing, and Althing-branded entries appear on the
// leaderboard. Panel testing with skeptical-executive personas showed this
// is the first credibility question serious readers ask, so we answer it
// affirmatively instead of leaving it to be discovered. Keep this factual:
// no marketing gloss, just the relationship and the mechanisms that keep
// the ranking honest despite it.

const mitigations = [
  {
    title: "Every score is recomputed",
    body: "The leaderboard never republishes a submitted aggregate. At publish time, open-source code (synthbench/publish.py) recomputes every row's SPS from its per-question distributions, including our own entries. Hand-editing a score cannot change a rank.",
  },
  {
    title: "Open code, public data",
    body: "The scoring code, the publish pipeline, the per-question artifacts, and the human reference distributions (minus the private holdout) are all public. Anyone can rebuild the leaderboard from the committed run files and check it against what we publish.",
  },
  {
    title: "Third-party submissions use the same pipeline",
    body: "External submissions go through the same validation, recomputation, run-validity filtering, and dedup logic as DataViking's own runs. There is no separate path. The pipeline is in the open repository, so any divergence would be visible.",
  },
  {
    title: "Private holdout applies to us too",
    body: "The verified/flagged badge compares each row's public-subset score against a 20% private holdout. Althing-branded rows are scored against the same hidden answer key as everyone else. Some of them are currently flagged, and we publish those flags.",
  },
  {
    title: "Unflattering results stay published",
    body: "Excluded runs are listed with reasons in the published data, ensemble caveats name our own defective constituent runs, and baseline floors are tracked in CI. These mechanisms make no exceptions for the operator's product.",
  },
];
---

<section class="w-full max-w-4xl mx-auto py-12 scroll-mt-24" id="operator-disclosure">
  <h2
    class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-2"
  >
    Who runs this
  </h2>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    SynthBench is built and operated by <strong class="text-text dark:text-text-dark">DataViking</strong>,
    the company that also makes
    <a
      href="https://github.com/DataViking-Tech/Althing"
      class="text-primary dark:text-primary-light hover:underline"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >Althing</a>, a synthetic-survey product. Althing-branded entries
    on the leaderboard are the operator's own product being scored on the
    operator's own benchmark. That is a conflict of interest, so the ranking
    should be checkable without relying on our word.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted mb-6 max-w-3xl">
    The benchmark is designed so that the operator gets no mechanical
    advantage:
  </p>

  <div class="space-y-4">
    {
      mitigations.map((m) => (
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-muted/20 bg-surface p-5 dark:bg-surface-dark">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-1">
            {m.title}
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">{m.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p class="text-muted mt-6 max-w-3xl text-sm">
    If you find a place where these mechanisms fall short, please
    <a
      href="https://github.com/DataViking-Tech/synthbench/issues"
      class="text-primary dark:text-primary-light hover:underline"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    > open an issue</a>. The benchmark is only useful if it is neutral.
  </p>
</section>
